      <span class="tg-hero__type">Topical Study · Five Parts</span>
      <h1 class="tg-hero__title">Assurance of Salvation</h1>
      <p class="tg-hero__intro">
        Can a believer know they are saved — and can a saved person be lost? Scripture
        gives promises that seem unbreakable and warnings that seem unanswerable, often
        in the same letter and occasionally in the same verse. This study follows the
        thread of salvation from Genesis to Revelation, reads the competing texts in
        their own contexts, states seven traditions in the form their own best
        theologians would recognise, sets out the tests Scripture actually gives, and
        ends with the ordinary means by which the fire is kept — and the road back for
        anyone who has let it go out.
      </p>
      <blockquote class="tg-hero__key-verse">
        <p>I write these things to you who believe in the name of the Son of God, that
           you may know that you have eternal life.</p>
        <cite><a class="ref" data-ref="1 John 5:13">1 John 5:13</a></cite>
      </blockquote>
    </div>
  </header><main>
    <div class="container">

      <section class="tg-section" id="why">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">Why This Question Is Difficult</h2>

        <p>It is not difficult because the Bible is unclear. It is difficult because
        the Bible says two things with equal force and declines to rank them.</p>

        <p>On one side: <em>no one will snatch them out of my hand</em>
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="John 10:28">John 10:28</a>); <em>he who began a good
        work in you will bring it to completion</em>
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="Philippians 1:6">Philippians 1:6</a>); <em>nothing
        in all creation will be able to separate us from the love of God</em>
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="Romans 8:39">Romans 8:39</a>).</p>

        <p>On the other: <em>let anyone who thinks that he stands take heed lest he
        fall</em> (<a class="ref" data-ref="1 Corinthians 10:12">1 Corinthians
        10:12</a>); <em>it is impossible... to restore them again to repentance</em>
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="Hebrews 6:4-6">Hebrews 6:4–6</a>); <em>I never knew
        you</em> (<a class="ref" data-ref="Matthew 7:23">Matthew 7:23</a>).</p>

        <p>Both sets are spoken to churches. Several of them come from the same author
        within a few pages. The author of Hebrews issues the sharpest warning in the
        New Testament and also writes that Christ "is able to save to the uttermost."
        Jude describes apostates for twenty-three verses and closes by blessing "him
        who is able to keep you from stumbling." Paul says <em>take heed lest you
        fall</em> and <em>God is faithful</em> in consecutive sentences.</p>

        <div class="tg-principle">
          <span class="tg-principle__label">The Study in One Paragraph</span>
          <p class="tg-principle__text">The security Scripture promises is real, and it
          is located outside you. The warnings Scripture issues are real, and they are
          addressed to you. These are not in competition — they are how the New
          Testament pastors people.</p>
          <p>The comfort never rests on the strength of your grip; every keeping text
          grounds it in the Father's giving, the Son's intercession, and the Spirit's
          sealing. And the warnings are not decoration: they are one of the instruments
          God uses to keep the people he has promised to keep. <strong>What is asked of
          the saved person has not changed since Genesis: to hold on to the God who has
          hold of you.</strong></p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="tg-section" id="parts">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">The Five Parts</h2>

        <p>Each part stands on its own, though they build. Parts I–III are the
        groundwork — biblical, exegetical, historical. Parts IV and V are the pastoral
        payload, and a reader in distress may reasonably start at Part IV.</p>

        <div class="tg-point">
          <span class="tg-point__label">Part I</span>
          <div class="tg-point__heading"><a href="part-1.html">The Thread from Eden to the New Covenant</a></div>
          <p>The single thread of salvation through the Hebrew Scriptures: God moving
          first in Eden; righteousness credited to Abraham's trust before circumcision
          and before law; rescue preceding the commandments at Sinai; the conditionality
          of Deuteronomy sitting beside the promise of a heart God himself circumcises;
          the wilderness generation; the remnant; Ezekiel's two opposite words; and
          Habakkuk 2:4 — the verse the New Testament quotes three times, and which
          contains both halves of this argument.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="tg-point">
          <span class="tg-point__label">Part II</span>
          <div class="tg-point__heading"><a href="part-2.html">The Competing Verses</a></div>
          <p>The keeping texts and the warning texts laid out in full and read in
          context. Close work on the six hardest passages — Hebrews 6 and 10,
          2 Peter 2, John 15, Galatians 5:4, Matthew 7 — including where each popular
          escape route fails. Then the five standard ways of holding them together with
          the price each one pays, the demonstration that three of them are one reading
          which carries every text without forcing any, and the verse pairs that were
          written to be read whole.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="tg-point">
          <span class="tg-point__label">Part III</span>
          <div class="tg-point__heading"><a href="part-3.html">The Schools of Thought</a></div>
          <p>Reformed, Lutheran, Wesleyan, Reformed Arminian, Roman Catholic, Orthodox,
          and Free Grace — each stated as its own advocates would state it, with its
          strongest text and its weakest link, the last applied evenly including to the
          position this study holds. Then the verdict, stated plainly rather than left
          to the reader — followed by the five things all seven agree on, and what each
          of them says to the person awake at two in the morning.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="tg-point">
          <span class="tg-point__label">Part IV</span>
          <div class="tg-point__heading"><a href="part-4.html">Testing Your Faith</a></div>
          <p>The tests Scripture gives — 1 John's three, 2 Peter's qualities, fruit,
          the negative lists — and six rules for running them so that they do what they
          were given to do. What "examine yourselves" actually meant in 2 Corinthians.
          The two opposite readers who need opposite medicine. The marks of false
          assurance, none of which is uncertainty. Why doubt is not unbelief. And a
          direct answer on the fear of the unforgivable sin.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="tg-point">
          <span class="tg-point__label">Part V</span>
          <div class="tg-point__heading"><a href="part-5.html">Keeping the Fire, and the Way Back</a></div>
          <p>How faith cools by drift rather than by decision. Ephesus, which had
          everything right and had put its first love down, and the three-step remedy
          Christ prescribes. The seven churches as a diagnostic — including the two he
          does not rebuke. The altar fire God lit and required a priest to feed every
          morning. The ordinary means. Why this cannot be done alone. And the road
          back, with the restorations Scripture narrates in detail so that nobody can
          claim their case is unprecedented.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="tg-section" id="method">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">A Note on Method</h2>

        <p>This study tries to do four things consistently. It states each position in
        the form its own best advocates would recognise rather than the form easiest to
        refute. It names the cost of every reading, including the readings it adopts.
        It keeps two questions apart that are constantly conflated — <em>can God's grip
        fail?</em> and <em>can I tell whether I am in his grip?</em> And it holds
        comfort and warning in the same tension Scripture holds them, without trading
        one away to simplify the other.</p>

        <div class="tg-note">
          <span class="tg-note__label">Where This Study Lands, Said Up Front</span>
          It reads apostasy the way 1 John 2:19 reads it, treats the warnings as God's
          genuine means of preserving genuine people, and works with the two-level
          covenant framework the Old Testament already uses. <strong>These are not
          three options to choose between — they answer three different questions, and
          together they carry every hard text in Part II without forcing one of
          them.</strong> Part II runs the whole list through the frame in a table so
          the claim can be checked rather than believed.<br><br>
          So this study does not end by saying every view has its problem verses and
          the reader must pick. It says the positions are not equally well supported
          and names which one reads the canon best — after stating all seven as their
          own advocates would state them, which is a duty owed to them and a different
          thing from declining to decide. What it does <em>not</em> claim is to have
          removed the mystery of how God's keeping and your responsibility interlock.
          Deuteronomy 30 commands a circumcised heart and promises God will perform it,
          in one speech. Ezekiel writes both chapter 18 and chapter 36. A reading that
          stops where Scripture stops has not failed; the systems with no mystery left
          got there by dropping half the canon.
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="tg-section" id="reader">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">Two Readers, and a Word to Each</h2>

        <p>An article like this one is read by two very different people, and the same
        sentences do opposite work in them. It is worth locating yourself before you
        begin.</p>

        <div class="tg-point">
          <span class="tg-point__label">If you are afraid</span>
          <div class="tg-point__heading">The warnings in this study are almost certainly not about you</div>
          <p>The people the New Testament warns are marked by hardness, contempt, and
          settled repudiation — never by anxiety. <strong>Fear about your standing is
          nowhere in Scripture listed as a mark of the lost.</strong> If you grieve over
          your sin, keep returning after failure, and want to obey even where you fail,
          you are displaying the evidence John wrote his letter to help you recognise.
          Part IV is written for you, and its rule 5 is the one to hold on to.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="tg-point">
          <span class="tg-point__label">If you are comfortable</span>
          <div class="tg-point__heading">There is no paragraph here that lets you off</div>
          <p>If your sin has become a matter of management rather than grief, if
          correction makes you angry rather than searching, if you have not spoken
          honestly to God in months and it has not troubled you — then the warnings are
          addressed to you, and not one of the seven traditions in Part III offers you a
          way around them. What all of them offer instead is the same thing: come back
          today, while you can still hear it. Part V is written for you.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="tg-section" id="preaching">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">Six Controls for Teaching This Subject</h2>

        <div class="tg-point">
          <span class="tg-point__label">For Teachers and Preachers</span>
          <p><strong>1. Preach both sets of texts, and preach them to the same
          room.</strong> The apostles did. A ministry that only comforts produces
          presumption; one that only warns produces despair. The New Testament's
          pattern is to warn severely and then say "but we are confident of better
          things in your case" — and both halves are said out loud.</p>
          <p><strong>2. Never use 2 Corinthians 13:5 on the anxious.</strong> Paul
          aimed it at people confident enough to be auditing an apostle, and he expected
          them to pass. Aimed at the frightened it does the opposite of its purpose.</p>
          <p><strong>3. Say plainly that assurance and salvation are not the same
          thing.</strong> A believer may lack the feeling for years without lacking the
          reality. Westminster says so; Wesley came to say so; nearly every tradition
          agrees, and congregations are rarely told.</p>
          <p><strong>4. Ground assurance outside the hearer before you offer any
          evidence from inside them.</strong> Evidence is genuinely useful and it is
          never the foundation. If the tests start functioning as the basis of standing
          rather than as a report on it, they have become law and they will condemn.</p>
          <p><strong>5. Preach the love test.</strong> Congregations examine themselves
          for sin and for orthodoxy; John presses love for the brothers hardest of the
          three and treats it as a ground of knowledge. It is frequently the most
          reassuring evidence a struggling believer has and has never been shown.</p>
          <p><strong>6. Give people someone.</strong> Hebrews 3:13 prescribes daily
          mutual exhortation because sin's characteristic effect is on the perception of
          the person doing it. Self-examination alone cannot detect a drifting
          instrument. Assurance is a community project before it is a private one.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

